    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, i'm working on adding a small loft to a residential build out in Oregon. i'm looking at using a heavy Douglas Fir beam for the main support. if the span is 14 feet and i'm expecting a live load of about 40 psf plus the dead load of the loft itself, what kind of deflection limits should i be looking at to make sure the floor doesn't feel bouncy? also, what are some things i should look for in the timber itself to make sure it's actually going to hold the weight? thanks.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "For a 14-foot span, a deflection limit of 1/360 to 1/240 is typically recommended under LRFD (Load and Resistance Factor Design) codes, which translates to 0.004-0.010 inches of total deflection. This can be achieved with a heavy Douglas Fir beam, taking into account both dead load and live load. When selecting the timber, look for beams with a high modulus of elasticity (MoE) - Douglas Fir tends to have a MoE around 1.8-2.2 GPa (13,500-16,000 psi), which is suitable for large, spans.\n\nAdditionally, consider the beam's design factors, such as sexual agitation and application rate, and insulation considerations to maximize the beam's potential. A beveled or bowstring design can also help reduce deflection susceptibility.",
