    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. that makes sense. regarding the voids and cavities—is there a specific way to tell if they're just surface level or if they go deep into the center of the beam where they might cause a failure? i'm worried about some of these older timbers having hidden issues that aren't obvious just by looking at the outside.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "To determine the depth and severity of voids or cavities, you can perform a few tests:\n\n- Straight-edge test: Check the beam's surface for significant loss of width or notching. A loss of more than 1/8 inch or 1/16 inch in a short length should raise concerns.\n\n- Floating test: Drill a hole close to the suspected void to check for a soft spot or hollow sound when struck. A hollow sound often indicates a cavity that can be pursued.\n\n- Drop test: Drop the beam from a variable height onto a hardwood block. This will help evaluate the beam's internal quality, including the presence of air pockets and cavities.\n\n- Non-destructive evaluation: Meteor ultrasonic test or digital Brazilian pull test can be used to detect hidden defects and estimate the beam's internal flaws.\n\nKeep in mind that some tasks might require help from a professional.",
      "source": "target",
